WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.999
Film Courage: What are the most common mistakes
new screenwriters make?

00:00:02.999 --> 00:00:06.200
Eric Edson: Okay, there’s a list.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:16.320
But the most common ones I see are 1) Not
enough story.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.670
Again it’s that old thing, characters can
be wonderful, dialogue can be sparkling or

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.300
even funny as heck.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:31.259
But if the story lays there just flat as a
pancake, that story is not going on anywhere.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:33.289
It’s a weak story.

00:00:33.289 --> 00:00:36.940
2) Would be passive central character.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.820
That’s just killed more scripts that just
about anything I’ve ever seen.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.100
And weak story and passive central character,
they go together.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.989
The really go together, fit together, too.

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:57.590
A passive central character is a hero or heroine,
the lead down the middle who doesn’t really

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:03.469
do anything in life and the story happens
to them.

00:01:03.469 --> 00:01:08.170
They do not happen to anything or anyone.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:16.930
And that is one way to put your audience to
sleep very, very quickly.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.500
Heroes have to be active.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.500
The hero is the audience.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.980
You build a story so that the hero is…with
lots of flaws and everything…fine, yes.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.640
Make them human.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:37.780
But they have certain qualities of character
and of circumstance.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.140
They have to be brave.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.430
That is absolutely essential.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.940
But you can put them in unfair injury.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.590
We care about people who are unjustly dealt
with.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:50.070
They are in a situation of danger.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:55.023
They’re funny, they have a sense of humor,
loved by other people (that always helps).

00:01:55.023 --> 00:02:00.670
There is this kind of list of things you can
do to keep characters interesting.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:07.850
That allows us to like them and once we start
seeing the nature of the hero of heroine’s

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:13.520
major problem and major, impossible task before
them.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:21.700
Hopefully on page 6 or 8, we the audience…the
audience becomes one person (each of us) sitting

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:22.700
alone.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:28.220
And we project ourselves, psychologically
project ourselves into the hero and then we’re

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.610
along for the ride.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:38.430
And if that hero doesn’t do anything, doesn’t
want anything and other people do all the

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:39.430
stuff.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:47.350
And all the people that come in, all the secondary
characters are the witty, fun, urbane, loquacious,

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:52.380
and joking and funny, but they’re all the
funny characters while your central character

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.700
is more or less wallpaper, it’s dead.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:02.250
And that is related to something else which
is the adversary.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:09.250
When that happens in a script, almost always
the problem is there is no adversary or no

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.411
powerful adversary.

00:03:10.411 --> 00:03:14.540
Adversary is so critical.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:18.830
People need to work on their adversary until
it is…The adversary should be the strongest

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.110
person in the story.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:25.480
You know when I was coming up they were like
“You know, they have to be equal in power

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:26.730
to the hero.”

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:27.730
No!

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.310
They have to be much more powerful than the
hero.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:37.090
It has to look like…for most of the movie…it
has to look like the hero has no bloody way

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:43.730
of defeating the adversary or surmounting
the problems being presented by this adversary,

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:46.390
but they do it.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:51.550
They outthink them and they out try them and
they can make it happen.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:57.760
And that’s a story with great conflict with
a hero who is doing things who takes us along

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.090
emotionally, that we can ride along happily
for the journey.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:05.060
And that’s how the good ones work.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:11.600
Where that’s probably more than two…I
threw in probably three or four.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:18.340
The next, the third I would say is getting
it out there too soon.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.220
That’s what destroys most screenplays.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:23.540
It’s hard!

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:31.070
You know, writing a screenplay, a feature
length screenplay, that’s hard (really hard).

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:36.720
And it’s not necessarily an all pleasant
undertaking labor.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.120
It hurts, you tear your hair out, you pound
your head against the wall, sometimes literally,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.220
but finally you’ve got a draft!

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.970
Oh, Gosh I did it!

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:47.720
Here it is…here it is.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:53.650
I’m going to get it out as quickly as I
can and that’s the time of great danger.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:05:00.400
You just can’t send it out, it is not ready
(guaranteed it is not ready).

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.640
Film Courage: Is there really this database
of scripts?

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:10.140
I mean really…it sounds like the medical
record database.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.490
Is there really this database of coverage
like “Ah, yes.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:14.490
Eric Edson.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.400
On this date he sent us this.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.680
No…no…the coverage was horrible.”

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.240
Eric Edson: Yes.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:21.860
Film Courage: Really this exists?

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.410
Eric Edson: Well…[exhales]…I have to confess
now.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:28.550
It’s been some years now since I was in
the thick of it, of this, but I don’t think

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:31.390
a whole lot has changed.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:32.900
Except they’ve gotten a lot more technological.

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:36.390
They’ve got better ways to keep track of
stuff.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:44.120
And…yeah…back then, you had basically
one shot because one…the person you are

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.840
sending it to (if you finally get an agent
to say “Okay.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:50.640
I’ll take a look and then leave me alone”).

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.509
Or if it’s a producer or a friend of a friend
or whatever.

00:05:54.509 --> 00:05:59.000
Most often, they are not going to read it,
they are going to hand it to their reader.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:04.150
And I remember those early on when I was getting
phone calls back producers and they were obviously

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:05.639
reading from notes.

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:10.200
They had obviously not read the script themselves,
they were working from reader’s notes.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.280
Okay, but they’ve got their time issues,
too.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.240
And they’ve got to use their time as best
they can.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:23.150
But, yeah…if you get a bad report first
time in, it is filed.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:30.750
Now it’s digitalized and you never know,
ultimately what company becomes joined with

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:35.360
what company and sometimes the people they
just know each other even if they are at different

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:36.360
companies.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.520
And “Did this pass through?

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.620
Send me over the report.”

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.250
And yes, those reports get disseminated.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.060
Film Courage: What if you change the name
of the script?

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:49.910
Let’s suppose you started one called ANGEL’S
FLIGHT and it got a pass and another pass.

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:54.360
So you decided to change it to BIRDS OF A
FEATHER (and I’m just totally making this

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:55.950
stuff up).

00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:00.781
So BIRDS OF A FEATHER has been looked at and
you’ve worked on it and you’ve had much

00:07:00.781 --> 00:07:06.330
more time but the story is very similar?

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:14.940
Eric Edson: I would say (my instinct would
say) that you’ve probably done severe, probably

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:16.790
irreparable damage to your career.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:18.450
Film Courage: To resend something?

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:19.450
Eric Edson: Yes.

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:20.450
Film Courage: Okay.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:23.220
Eric Edson: Under a different title because
they have the plot.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:26.870
That’s one of the things a reader does,
they outline the entire plot before they say

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:28.020
“Ahh?”

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:33.820
Whether good, bad or indifferent.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:35.570
No…don’t do it.

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:38.560
Don’t just go around changing titles.

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:42.900
Because they don’t like it, they don’t
trust it.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.330
Film Courage: They don’t trust you?

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.740
Ahh, so that’s the bottom line.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:51.180
Eric Edson: If you really worked on something,
first of all, let time pass.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.470
You’re not going to be like “Ooooh!

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:55.960
I’ve rewritten it and it’s really, really
good!”

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:58.180
It’s 5 months later and “No!

00:07:58.180 --> 00:07:59.340
Forget it.”

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.169
Put it away.

00:08:01.169 --> 00:08:06.919
And then when the times comes and then what
they are looking for on any given year, you

00:08:06.919 --> 00:08:13.570
know the wheel keeps turning, the nature of
the material they are looking for.

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:15.820
And then, do it honestly.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:24.090
Say “Look, I’ve tried this out on you
two, three year’s ago.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.320
I still believe in the idea and I’ve been
working on it and I think it’s a great deal

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:29.320
better.”

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:33.690
Or you go to somebody who hasn’t ready it
before, because there is always someone who

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:44.570
hasn’t read it and you say a friend of your’s
(Nancy) she quite liked it or find a back

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:49.120
door the the friend, maybe Nancy?

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.960
But I wouldn’t change the title.

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.760
Film Courage: So be upfront, play by their
rules.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:54.760
Eric Edson: Be upfront.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:55.760
Film Courage: Because it could hurt you.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:58.250
Eric Edson: Yeah, you need to be upfront about
it.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:02.120
Film Courage: Why do you think people are
scared to be upfront?

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:08.500
Eric Edson: Because sometimes liars get away
with it.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:09.500
We have examples.

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:10.500
We have examples.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:11.500
Film Courage: We do.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.360
But we don’t have to go into them, but we
have plenty.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:19.110
Eric Edson: Sometimes liars get away with
it but sometimes people in this business,

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:25.870
I’m not saying they’re all greatly talented,
but a lot of them are, a lot of them are enormously,

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:33.950
they just have different talents and creativity
and they mean well.

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:41.570
They’re not inherently dishonest or scuzzy
people or anything like that.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:51.071
But they make mistakes and you need to learn
from their mistakes and also (amazingly) they

00:09:51.071 --> 00:09:52.420
have long memories, too.

